a momentary hitch today for tens of  millions of Facebook users forced to log  in to their normally easy access  accounts what appeared like a minor  inconvenience was for Facebook a major  problem on Tuesday the social media  giant discovered that almost 50 million  of its users were exposed by a security  flaw which Facebook revealed on Friday  meant attackers could have gained  control of the accounts the  vulnerability was in Facebook's view as  feature a security feature that lets  people see what their own profile looks  like to someone else it exposed your  access token the digital key that keeps  you locked into your account without  having to re-enter the password hackers  could then steal that access token to  take over your account  Facebook's reset the account that thinks  were affected fifty million and another  forty million as an added precaution  effectively changing the locks so the  stolen keys no longer work it's also  temporarily turned off view as but the  problems might not stop there  interestingly one of the main comments  coming out is the ripple effect or the  potential ripple effect because your  facebook login can be used to access all  of other platforms so people might be  using it for Instagram tinder those kind  of platforms and as yet there's no  clarity or whether or not those other  platforms might therefore have been  compromised through this particular  breach Facebook traced the hack back to  a change in its video code in July 2017  meaning attackers had access to people's  accounts for over a year  what were the hackers doing Facebook  still isn't sure but it's working with  the FBI to find out and the UK's  national cybersecurity Center is  investigating how the incident affected  UK users the firm said people's privacy  and security is incredibly important and  we're sorry this happened it's why we've  taken immediate action to secure digits  and let users know what happened there's  no need for anyone to change their  passwords the locks have been changed  but the burglars have already been  inside now 50 million users  we'll wait to find out what might have  happened while Facebook was sleeping  Rodman Thorpe Sky News
 

